<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hebron</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hebron is a city in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>, in the Judaean hills about 30 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. It is revered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Christians and Muslims as the burial place of Abraham and the patriarchs at the Cave of the Patriarchs, enclosed by a Herodian wall. According to the Bible, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> was first made king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> there before moving his capital to Jerusalem. It is now the largest Palestinian city in the West Bank.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hebron.jpg" alt="Hebron" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oren Rozen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BTS_Hebron_Tour_280215_24.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
